---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'bigfiles', title: 'bigfiles — Largest Files' },
  { id: 'dupes', title: 'dupes — Duplicate Hunter' },
  { id: 'du', title: 'du — Folder Sizes' },
  { id: 'analyze', title: 'analyze — Read-Only Treemap' },
  { id: 'devscan', title: 'devscan — Developer Cruft' },
  { id: 'deepscan', title: 'deepscan — Pattern Hunt' },
  { id: 'leftovers', title: 'leftovers — Uninstalled Apps' },
  { id: 'list-first', title: 'The List-First Contract' }
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Disk Intelligence Tools"
  description="bigfiles, dupes, du, analyze, devscan, deepscan and leftovers — the discovery and reclamation commands that go beyond the built-in cleaners."
  currentSlug="disk-intelligence"
  toc={toc}
  prevPage={{ title: 'Scheduling & Snapshots', href: '/docs/automation' }}
  nextPage={{ title: 'Residue Engine', href: '/docs/residue' }}
>
  <div class="space-y-10">
    <!-- Overview -->
    <section id="overview">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Overview</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        The built-in cleaners handle known applications. The disk-intelligence commands handle everything else: the unknown space hog, the duplicate library, the abandoned build tree. They share two properties — they are read-only until you pass <code class="text-cyan">--clean</code> (or, for <code class="text-cyan">deepscan</code>, until you omit <code class="text-cyan">--preview</code>), and they all estimate sizes before touching anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- bigfiles -->
    <section id="bigfiles">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">bigfiles — Largest Files</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Ranks the biggest files first, with an optional age filter so you can target things nobody has opened in months.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep bigfiles --min-size 500MB --top 20
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep bigfiles ~/Downloads --older-than 90d --clean</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- dupes -->
    <section id="dupes">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">dupes — Duplicate Hunter</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A two-tier comparison — exact byte size first, then a content hash — confirms true duplicates, not files that merely share a name. Two flags make the result predictable:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-text-muted mb-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-cyan">▸</span><span><strong><code class="text-cyan">--keep</code></strong> chooses the survivor per group: <code class="text-cyan">first</code>, <code class="text-cyan">newest</code>, <code class="text-cyan">oldest</code>, <code class="text-cyan">largest</code> or <code class="text-cyan">smallest</code>. The tie-break is deterministic and documented, and an unreadable file never wins.</span></li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-cyan">▸</span><span><strong><code class="text-cyan">--link</code></strong> replaces duplicates with hard links to the kept copy instead of deleting them, using a rename-link-unlink sequence that fails safe across filesystems.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep dupes ~/Pictures --min-size 1MB --keep newest
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep dupes ~/Pictures --link --yes</code></pre>
      <p class="text-xs text-text-dim mt-3">Overlapping scan roots never list the same path twice.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- du -->
    <section id="du">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">du — Folder Sizes</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Parallel per-child recursive sizes for one directory. Strictly read-only — it never deletes, and it powers the drill-down Disk screen in the GUI.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep du ~ --top 15</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- analyze -->
    <section id="analyze">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">analyze — Read-Only Treemap</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A directory-and-file ranking for when you want a map rather than a single number. Also read-only.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep analyze ~ --top 15</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- devscan -->
    <section id="devscan">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">devscan — Developer Cruft</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Finds regenerable build output — <code class="text-cyan">node_modules</code>, <code class="text-cyan">target/</code>, <code class="text-cyan">.gradle</code>, <code class="text-cyan">.venv</code>, <code class="text-cyan">__pycache__</code> — but only when the surrounding project proves it is safe: a <code class="text-cyan">package.json</code> or <code class="text-cyan">Cargo.toml</code> must be present. A folder merely named <code class="text-cyan">build</code> is not enough.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep devscan ~/projects --min-size 50MB
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep devscan ~/projects --clean --yes</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- deepscan -->
    <section id="deepscan">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">deepscan — Pattern Hunt</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A glob-based junk hunt over roots you choose (defaults from the config). Unlike most commands here it is destructive unless you pass <code class="text-cyan">--preview</code>, so preview it by default.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep deepscan /tmp --preview
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep deepscan ~/Downloads --pattern "*.tmp" --preview</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- leftovers -->
    <section id="leftovers">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">leftovers — Uninstalled Apps</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Hunts state left behind by an app that is no longer installed, and verifies with the local package manager that no active package still owns the directory before it is offered for deletion.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep leftovers discord
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep leftovers discord --clean</code></pre>
      <p class="text-xs text-text-dim mt-3">
        For context-aware scanning with confidence scores, see the <a href="/docs/residue" class="text-cyan hover:underline">Residue Engine</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- List-first -->
    <section id="list-first">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The List-First Contract</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        <code class="text-cyan">leftovers</code>, <code class="text-cyan">devscan</code>, <code class="text-cyan">dupes</code> and <code class="text-cyan">bigfiles</code> only report matches until you pass <code class="text-cyan">--clean</code>, and even then the preview is shown and confirmation is required — pass <code class="text-cyan">--yes</code> for scripts. The user keep list is respected by every one of these scans, and <code class="text-cyan">--force</code> cannot override it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
